    subject: "reading_writing",
    domain: `Information and Ideas`,
    skill: `Central Ideas and Details`,
    difficulty: `Hard`,
    text: `In episodes of high heat, corals expel symbiotic zooxanthellae from their tissues, leading to coral bleaching and heightened coral mortality. Some
researchers have proposed a mitigation strategy of identifying, rearing, and disseminating corals that have evolved strong resistance to heat, but
as Nia S. Walker et al. note, if genes conferring such resistance were exclusively advantageous, they should have already propagated through
coral populations. Indeed, Walker et al. show that strongly heat-resistant corals display much less growth after heat stress than do moderately
heat-resistant corals.
Based on the text, Walker et al. would most likely agree with which statement about the strategy some researchers have proposed?`,
    type: "multiple_choice",
    choices: [
      `A. It would involve promoting a trait that protects corals against high heat but that is associated with reduced thriving after episodes of high
heat.`,
      `B. It would favor a trait that is advantageous now but would be disadvantageous if episodes of high heat become as infrequent as has been
predicted.`,
      `C. It would benefit corals that are frequently exposed to episodes of high heat but would harm corals that are frequently exposed to episodes of
moderate heat.`,
      `D. It would require manipulating a trait that is poorly understood and that may be linked to increased coral mortality during episodes of high heat.`,
    ],
    correctAnswer: `A`,
    explanation: `Choice A is the best answer because it presents a statement about the proposed strategy that is consistent with Walker et al.’s position. The
strategy involves identifying and disseminating corals with strong heat resistance as a means of mitigating coral mortality during episodes of
high heat. According to the text, Walker et al. note that if this trait were “exclusively advantageous,” it would have already spread throughout coral
populations—implying that the trait carries some evolutionary cost. The text then provides evidence of this cost: Walker et al. have found that
strongly heat-resistant corals display much less growth after heat stress (indicative of reduced thriving) than do moderately heat-resistant corals.
Given the apparent trade-off between strong heat resistance and growth after heat stress, Walker et al. would be likely to agree that the strategy
entails promoting a trait that protects against high heat but is associated with reduced thriving afterward.
Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn’t suggest that episodes of high heat are predicted to become infrequent. Also, the text establishes
that Walker et al. found that the trait of high heat tolerance is associated with a significant trade-off—reduced growth after heat stress. Given the
cost that accompanies strong heat resistance, Walker et al. wouldn’t be likely to agree that the trait is advantageous now. Choice C is incorrect
because the text doesn’t discuss the effects of moderate heat on corals or distinguish between corals that are exposed to different levels of
heat. The text also doesn’t address the frequency of corals’ exposure to heat. Therefore, there is no basis for concluding that Walker et al. would
agree that the strategy benefits corals that are frequently exposed to high heat but harms those that are frequently exposed to moderate heat.
Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn’t indicate that heat resistance in corals is poorly understood. On the contrary, Walker et al. present a
specific finding about this trait, linking reduced growth after heat stress to strong heat resistance. In addition, the text indicates that strong heat
resistance protects corals during episodes of high heat; while their research shows that heat resistance is linked to reduced growth after such
episodes, Walker et al. likely wouldn’t agree that it is linked to increased coral mortality during such episodes.`,
