    domain: `Information and Ideas`,
    skill: `Central Ideas and Details`,
    difficulty: `Hard`,
    text: `The following text is adapted from William Shakespeare’s 1597 play The Tragedy of King Richard III. Richard is reflecting on the recent arrest of
his brother, the Duke of Clarence, on suspicion of treason against King Edward IV. Derby, Hastings, Buckingham, Rivers, Dorset, and Grey are also
members of the English nobility.
RICHARD: I do the wrong, and first begin to brawl.
The secret mischiefs that I set [flowing]
I lay unto the grievous charge of others.
Clarence, whom I indeed have cast in darkness,
I do beweep to many simple [gullible people],
Namely, to Derby, Hastings, Buckingham;
And tell them ’tis the Queen and her allies
That stir the King against the Duke my brother.
Now they believe it, and withal whet me
To be revenged on Rivers, Dorset, Grey.
Which choice best describes what happens in the text?`,
    type: "multiple_choice",
    choices: [
      `A. Richard describes having wept as he informed Derby, Hastings, and Buckingham that the queen and her allies convinced the king to act
against Clarence, and says that the earnestness of his grief caused them to accept his version of events.`,
      `B. Richard attributes Clarence’s troubles to both his own secret plotting and the distrust of Clarence that the queen and her allies Derby, Hastings,
and Buckingham have planted in the king’s mind.`,
      `C. Richard indicates that he has pretended to be aggrieved about Clarence’s situation and has proclaimed it to be the fault of the queen and her
allies, but in reality, he has caused the hostility the king feels toward Clarence.`,
      `D. Richard acknowledges that his mischievous nature has spurred him to commit misdeeds in the past, including instigating enmity between the
king and Clarence, but he reports that he has hitherto not lost the trust of the queen and her allies.`,
    ],
    correctAnswer: `C`,
    explanation: `Choice C is the best answer because it accurately describes what happens in the text. Richard admits he committed “the wrong” and blames
others for his own “secret mischiefs.” He reveals that he has “cast [Clarence] in darkness” (that is, caused his imprisonment) but feigns grief—“I
do beweep”—before “many simple [gullible people],” namely Derby, Hastings, and Buckingham. He tells them that the queen and her allies are the
ones who turned the king against Clarence. Richard’s use of “beweep” and his labeling of his audience as gullible confirm that his grief is a
performance. Thus, Richard pretends to be aggrieved and blames the queen while actually being responsible for Clarence’s predicament.
Choice A is incorrect because it characterizes Richard’s grief as earnest, claiming that his sincere grief moved others to accept his claims.
However, Richard admits his wrongdoing and calls his audience “simple [gullible people],” making clear that they were deceived by a calculated
performance rather than persuaded by genuine emotion. Rather than being sincere, Richard’s weeping is part of a deliberate scheme to blame
the queen and her allies for what he did. Choice B is incorrect because Richard does not attribute any portion of Clarence’s troubles to the queen;
he reveals that he “cast [Clarence] in darkness” and merely tells others the queen is responsible. Additionally, Derby, Hastings, and Buckingham
are not identified as the queen’s allies; rather, Rivers, Dorset, and Grey are implied to be the queen’s allies. Choice D is incorrect because the text
does not address whether Richard has maintained the trust of the queen and her allies. Instead, Richard tells Derby, Hastings, and Buckingham
that the queen is to blame for Clarence’s troubles, and they in turn urge him to seek revenge on Rivers, Dorset, and Grey, who are implied to be the
queen’s allies. The speech focuses on Richard’s manipulation of others, not on his standing with the queen.`,
