    subject: "reading_writing",
    domain: `Information and Ideas`,
    skill: `Central Ideas and Details`,
    difficulty: `Medium`,
    text: `Given the immense scope of space, the search for extraterrestrial life is almost necessarily concentrated on the exoplanets deemed to have the
most plausible chance of success—typically, atmosphere-bearing terrestrial planets orbiting within a certain range of their stars (termed the
habitable zone). Claiming that Earth experienced a long transition from single-lid to plate tectonics that accelerated the emergence and evolution
of complex organisms, researchers Robert J. Stern and Taras V. Gerya hold that consideration of tectonics, an often overlooked factor, could help
further narrow the search for advanced extraterrestrial species.
Based on the text, what do Stern and Gerya most likely believe about the development of complex life on exoplanets?`,
    type: "multiple_choice",
    choices: [
      `A. It is more likely to occur on habitable zone planets with atmospheres and plate tectonics than on otherwise similar planets that lack plate
tectonics.`,
      `B. It is more likely to occur if habitable zone planets with atmospheres transition from single-lid to plate tectonics late in their history than if they
transition early in their history.`,
      `C. It is unlikely unless the transition from single-lid to plate tectonics occurs before the acquisition of a lasting atmosphere.`,
      `D. It is probably more dependent on the presence of plate tectonics than on orbital distance from a host star or the presence of an atmosphere.`,
    ],
    correctAnswer: `A`,
    explanation: `Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes what Stern and Gerya most likely believe about the development of complex
life on exoplanets. The text indicates that the search for extraterrestrial life already focuses on atmosphere-bearing planets in the habitable zone
because their conditions are best suited for complex organisms. Stern and Gerya claim that plate tectonics on Earth accelerated the emergence
of complex organisms, and they therefore hold that tectonics could “help further narrow the search.” This phrase implies that plate tectonics
should be added to the existing criteria. In other words, the text implies that among habitable zone planets with atmospheres, those that also
have plate tectonics would be more likely to enable the development of complex life than otherwise similar planets lacking plate tectonics.
Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn’t address whether the timing of the transition to plate tectonics—early versus late in a planet’s
history—affects the likelihood of complex life. Although the text mentions that plate tectonics emerged on Earth after a “long transition” and that
this process accelerated the development of complex life, it presents these details as context for why plate tectonics matter, not as a claim
about when the transition would have needed to occur within the planet’s history. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn’t discuss the
timing of the acquisition of an atmosphere in relation to that of the transition to plate tectonics or explain why this would be relevant to the
development of complex life. Rather, the text presents these as independent factors relevant to the search for extraterrestrial life. Choice D is
incorrect because the text presents plate tectonics as an additional factor to consider alongside orbital distance from a host star and
atmosphere, not as a more important one. Stern and Gerya propose that tectonics could “help further narrow the search,” which implies
supplementing, rather than superseding, the existing criteria.`,
